**Answer: Yes, $a = b$ is necessary.**

## Proof

Let $L(x) = \lfloor a\lfloor bx\rfloor\rfloor$ and $R(x) = \lfloor b\lfloor ax\rfloor\rfloor$. The hypothesis is $L(x)=R(x)$ for all $x>0$.

**Step functions and their jumps.** On $[n/b,(n+1)/b)$ ($n=0,1,2,\dots$) we have $\lfloor bx\rfloor=n$, so $L(x)=\lfloor an\rfloor$; thus $L$ jumps at $x=n/b$ ($n\ge1$) by
$$\lambda_n:=\lfloor an\rfloor-\lfloor a(n-1)\rfloor.$$
Likewise $R(x)=\lfloor bm\rfloor$ on $[m/a,(m+1)/a)$, jumping at $x=m/a$ ($m\ge1$) by
$$\rho_m:=\lfloor bm\rfloor-\lfloor b(m-1)\rfloor.$$
Since $f:=L-R\equiv0$ is a step function, every net jump must be $0$.

**Lemma 1. $a/b\in\mathbb{Q}$.**
If $a/b$ were irrational, then $n/b\neq m/a$ for all positive integers $n,m$, so all jump points are distinct. At $x=n/b$ only $L$ jumps, forcing $\lambda_n=0$ for every $n\ge1$. But $\sum_{n=1}^{N}\lambda_n=\lfloor aN\rfloor\to\infty$, contradiction. $\square$

Write $a/b=p/q$ with $\gcd(p,q)=1$, so $a=bp/q$. The common jump points satisfy $n/b=m/a\iff n=kq,\;m=kp$ ($k\ge1$). Hence:

- **(A)** $\lambda_n=0$ whenever $q\nmid n$;
- **(B)** $\rho_m=0$ whenever $p\nmid m$.

Since $a$ is irrational, $an\notin\mathbb{Z}$, and $\lambda_n$ counts integers in $(a(n-1),an]$. An integer $j\ge1$ lies there iff $n=\lceil j/a\rceil$. So:

$$\text{(A)}\iff q\mid\lceil j/a\rceil\ \text{for all }j\ge1,\qquad \text{(B)}\iff p\mid\lceil j/b\rceil\ \text{for all }j\ge1.$$

**Case 1: $a>1$ and $b>1$.** (Irrationality gives $a,b>1$ strictly.) $\lambda_1=\lfloor a\rfloor\ge1$, so (A) forces $q\mid1$, i.e.\ $q=1$. Likewise $\rho_1\ge1$ forces $p=1$. Thus $a/b=1$, so $a=b$.

**Case 2: $a>1,\; b<1$.** Again $q=1$, so $a=bp$. Condition (B): $p\mid\lceil j/b\rceil$ for all $j$. Since $j/b=jp/a$,
$$\lceil jp/a\rceil=p\lfloor j/a\rfloor+\lceil p\{j/a\}\rceil,$$
which is divisible by $p$ iff $\lceil p\{j/a\}\rceil=p$, i.e.\ $\{j/a\}>(p-1)/p$. This must hold for **all** $j\ge1$. But $1/a$ is irrational, so by **Weyl's equidistribution theorem** $\{j/a\}$ is dense in $[0,1]$—contradiction.

**Case 3: $a<1,\; b>1$.** Symmetrically $\rho_1\ge1$ forces $p=1$, $a=b/q$. Condition (A) yields $\{j/b\}>(q-1)/q$ for all $j$, contradicting equidistribution of $\{j/b\}$ (since $1/b$ is irrational).

**Case 4: $a<1,\; b<1$.** From (A): $q\mid\lceil j/a\rceil$. With $j/a=jq/(bp)$,
$$\lceil jq/(bp)\rceil=q\bigl\lfloor j/(bp)\bigr\rfloor+\bigl\lceil q\{j/(bp)\}\bigr\rceil,$$
so divisibility by $q$ requires $\{j/(bp)\}>(q-1)/q$. From (B) similarly $\{j/(bp)\}>(p-1)/p$. Hence
$$\{j/(bp)\}>\delta:=\max\!\bigl((q-1)/q,\,(p-1)/p\bigr)<1\quad\text{for all }j\ge1.$$
Yet $\beta:=1/(bp)$ is irrational (as $b$ is), so $\{j\beta\}$ is equidistributed and dense—contradiction.

**Conclusion.** Every possible case yields either $a=b$ or a contradiction. Therefore $a=b$ is necessary. $\blacksquare$

